<div class="overview" data-controller="prototype-overview">
  <div class="intro">
    <h1>PROTOTYPE · Live updates without leaking hands</h1>
    <p>
      How do live updates reach each Player instantly without ever sending them another Player's cards?
      Below are the four seats of one Game, each in its own iframe with its own cable connection.
      Play a card in the highlighted hand, or press <em>Autoplay</em>.
      Flip variants with the bar at the bottom or the ← → keys.
    </p>

    <div class="variant">
      <% case @variant %>
      <% when "A" %>
        <strong>A · Turbo Streams per Player.</strong>
        Each seat subscribes to its own signed stream (<code>turbo_stream_from :game, player</code>).
        On every move the server renders the table four times, once per viewer, and sends each Player its own HTML.
        <br>Classic bug: render the table once, for the Player who moved, and send it to everyone, as with one shared Game stream.
      <% when "B" %>
        <strong>B · Turbo page refresh.</strong>
        All four seats subscribe to one shared Game stream that only ever carries <code>&lt;turbo-stream action="refresh"&gt;</code>.
        Each browser then re-fetches its own page over HTTP, rendered for whoever is signed in, and morphs it in.
        The cable never carries a card, so the bug toggle has nothing to break.
        Turbo waits <code>pageRefreshDebouncePeriod</code> after a refresh arrives, 150 ms by default, before fetching.
        <br>Catch-up: each subscription carries the version its page was rendered at, and on every (re)subscription
        the server sends that browser a refresh if the Game has moved on. Try <em>Drop connection for 5s</em> on a seat, then play.
      <% when "C" %>
        <strong>C · Custom channel + Stimulus.</strong>
        A custom <code>Prototype::LiveUpdatesChannel</code> picks each seat's stream from the connection's identity and sends it a JSON view.
        A Stimulus controller re-renders the table in JavaScript, a second renderer next to the ERB one. Cards are played over the socket.
        <br>Classic bug: serialize the Game with its hands and let the browser pick out its own. The table still looks right; only the wire gives it away.
      <% end %>
    </div>

    <p>
      Cable adapter: <code><%= @cable["adapter"] %></code>
      <% if @cable["polling_interval"] %>polling every <code><%= @cable["polling_interval"] %></code><% end %>
      (run <code>bin/prototype-live-updates async</code> to compare with the in-process adapter).
      Latency is measured from the server committing a move to each browser rendering it.
    </p>

    <div class="controls">
      <%= button_to "Deal again", prototype_live_updates_deal_path %>
      <%= button_to "Autoplay the rest of the Deal", prototype_live_updates_autoplay_path %>
      <%= form_with url: prototype_live_updates_settings_path, method: :patch do |form| %>
        <label><%= form.check_box :bug, { checked: @state["bug"] }, "1", "0" %> Classic bug</label>
        <% if @variant == "B" %>
          <label>Refresh debounce <%= form.select :refresh_debounce, [ [ "150 ms (Turbo default)", 150 ], [ "0 ms", 0 ] ], selected: @state["refresh_debounce"] %></label>
        <% else %>
          <%= form.hidden_field :refresh_debounce, value: @state["refresh_debounce"] %>
        <% end %>
        <%= form.submit "Apply and reload seats" %>
      <% end %>
    </div>

    <table class="summary">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Seat</th><th>updates</th><th>last ms</th><th>median ms</th><th>p95 ms</th><th>max ms</th>
          <th>cable frames</th><th>cable KB</th><th>HTTP fetches</th><th>HTTP KB</th><th>cards from other hands</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody data-prototype-overview-target="rows"></tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="seats">
    <% %w[north east west south].each do |seat| %>
      <iframe src="<%= prototype_live_updates_seat_path(seat) %>" title="<%= seat %>"></iframe>
    <% end %>
  </div>

  <%= render "prototype/switcher", variants: Prototype::LiveUpdatesController::VARIANTS, current: @variant %>
</div>
